# Implement — etude-1od: docs stages in run manifests

## What shipped

Two regressions and a runbook mapping. No production code changed, because the
capability already works — what was missing was anything pinning it or telling a
supervisor how to use it.

**`TestGateStageResolvesDocsStageByRoleNotName`.** A run whose stage is named
`docs` and produces `docs-diff` is gateable at `--stage docs`, and the recorded
`reviewed_stages` entry carries both the stage name and the role.

This is deliberately not a copy of the existing
`TestGateStageRunWithoutReviewableStageIsAClearError`, which pins the generic
"no stage for this role" path — the opus seat correctly warned that a duplicate
would be a rename, not a regression. What this pins is the thing that PRODUCED
the bead: resolution keys on the produced ROLE, and `docs` is the only stage in
the workflow whose role differs from its name.

Negative control: switching resolution from `req.Stage.Produces` to
`req.Stage.Name` fails this test and nothing else in the suite — every other
stage would still pass, which is exactly how the original defect stayed
invisible.

**`TestGateStageDocsWithoutDocsDiffStageWritesNothing`.** The half the generic
test does not cover: on the failure path the run ref is left completely
untouched — no gate attempt, no stage change. The original defect was
`capture-gate` REJECTING a gate whose `reviewed_stages` named an absent stage;
`etude gate` has to refuse upstream without leaving a partial attempt behind.

The fixture is a run with a stage NAMED `docs` that produces the wrong role —
the case a name-keyed resolver would wrongly accept.

**`docs/plans/dogfood/supervisor-runbook.md`.** A literal, copy-pasteable
`etude capture docs … --output docs-diff=…` line, with the reason it exists: four
of five stages produce a role that tracks their name, `docs` produces
`docs-diff`, and a supervisor pattern-matching the other four writes
`--output docs=…` and then meets "no reviewable stage on run". The runbook
already listed `docs-diff` among the roles; the seat's point was that a
restatement pays for nothing and only a command does.

## Why no production change

The bead names `scripts/dogfood-capture.sh`, which bead `.3` deletes. Both
plan-gate seats accepted resolving the defect in the model instead, taking the
bead's own second branch ("define a lossless supported mapping"). Recorded on the
bead so the tracker does not contradict this commit.

`etude capture-run` needed no change either: I checked rather than assumed — its
only stage-name check is `validateCLIIdentifier`, a generic identifier rule with
no allow-list. The four-stage limitation lived in the dogfood script alone.

## Checks

`make test` green · `make lint` green · `make docs-check` green ·
`make docs-reality` OK, 15 commands.

Live evidence this is true in practice and not only in tests:
`etude run show etude-9uf.2` lists a `docs` stage with `role=docs-diff`, and its
run ref carries `gate: docs.r1` attached to it.
